# RosettaNode encoding experiment

This directory implements `decode_prefix`, `decode_exact`, `serialize`, and
`identify` from the supplied `interface.md` and `packet.md`. It uses only the Go
standard library and has no external module dependencies.

Build and run:

```sh
go build -o rosettanode .
./rosettanode
```

The program reads one JSON object per line from stdin, flushes one JSON response
per line to stdout, and sends process diagnostics to stderr. A final line without
a newline is accepted. Oversized lines are drained before reading the next
request. Blank lines return `invalid_request`.

Run the local tests:

```sh
go test ./...
go vet ./...
go test -run '^$' -fuzz '^FuzzDecode$' -fuzztime=10s -parallel=1 .
```

The tests include the supplied encoding/hash and prefix examples, hand-written
witness encodings, CompactSize boundaries, signed amount bit patterns, error
precedence, item and byte envelopes, line framing, constant-scratch scanning,
and generated transaction round trips. The fuzz target checks that accepted
prefixes reconstruct and malformed bytes do not cause execution failures.

## Interpretations of unspecified details

- Decimal strings use a canonical spelling: negative zero is rejected.
- Optional fields may be absent; explicit `null` is rejected where a typed
  value is required. Duplicate JSON keys use the last value, as in Go's JSON
  decoder. Unknown fields in `limits` are ignored; transaction, input, and
  output object field sets remain exact.
- The structured byte pool includes all previous digests, scripts, and witness
  payloads, even when witness serialization is disabled. Witness items also
  count toward the structured item budget in that case. Ignored envelope
  fields contribute only to the JSON text limit.
- Admission applies to the entire byte suffix beginning at the prefix offset,
  even when a shorter transaction could be decoded from that suffix. An
  offset at the end of the byte string is allowed as a position, then decoding
  returns `malformed_encoding` because no transaction remains.
- JSON text transport limits are checked before parsing. After text parsing,
  invalid typed fields and offsets precede byte-pool transport limits; byte
  transport precedes admission and item limits. Structured field validation
  completes before the byte-pool and item-budget verdicts. Decode scanning and
  exact trailing-byte rejection precede the item-budget verdict, as required.
- The serialized size cap applies to the requested serialization. Stripped
  serialization can succeed when only the full encoding would exceed 4 MiB;
  `identify` requires both encodings to fit.
- Line-ending LF and optional CR are framing and do not count toward the
  20 MiB JSON text limit.

Only the supplied materials and local tests were used. No external sources,
reference implementations, evaluator access, agents, or network tools were used.
